/-!
# W70-CFOUR: the graded layer `γ₃/γ₄`, on the tower

Wave 69 reduced the class-four clearing obligation to an identity in `Y = λ₂/λ₄` and recorded
that what it lacked was a graded-layer description of `γ₃/γ₄` **on the tower**: the tree carries
that kind of material only on the concrete slices (`GradedStageFour*`, `GradedFive*`, the
`SqU5`/`SqU6` carriers).  This file supplies the tower version, for an arbitrary topological
group, in the three forms a class-four run consumes.

Throughout `Q₄ = levelQuot G 4`, `Y = lambdaImage G 2 4` is the image of `γ₂` and
`Z = zLayer G 3` is the image of `γ₃`.  Wave 69 established that `Y` is abelian in `Q₄`
(`cfour_commP_lambdaTwo`), that `Z` is central of exponent two, and that a bracket or a square
of a `Y`-element already lies in `Z`.  So `Z` is an elementary abelian 2-group receiving two
maps out of `Y`, and the whole of `γ₃/γ₄` is what those two maps generate.

## §1 The two homomorphisms `Y → Z`

Wave 69's `cfour_commP_left_hom` / `cfour_commP_right_hom` give bimultiplicativity against a
`Y`-slot.  The dual statements, with the `Y`-slot **inside** the bracket rather than opposite
it, are what a computation with `γ₂`-valued corrections actually needs, and they hold for the
same reason: the correction term of the free identity is then a bracket against a `Z`-element.
`gThreeFour_ad_left`, `gThreeFour_ad_right`, and the two inverse laws
`gThreeFour_ad_inv_left`, `gThreeFour_ad_inv_right`.

`gThreeFour_commP_sq_left` records the one place a square in the near slot survives:
`commP (p ^ 2) q = (commP p q) ^ 2 * commP (commP p q) p`, the group form of
`ad (x ^ [2]) = ad x ∘ ad x` in a restricted Lie algebra.  Compare `cfour_commP_sq_left`, which
says the same expression dies when the *far* slot is a `Y`-element.

## §2 Jacobi

⭐⭐ `gThreeFour_jacobi`: in `Q₄`,

```text
  commP (commP p q) r * commP (commP q r) p * commP (commP r p) q = 1 .
```

The tree has no Jacobi identity and no Hall-Witt identity anywhere; the subgroup-level
three-subgroups lemma (`commutator_commutator_le_of_rotate`) is the only relative of it, and it
is too coarse to move a single element.  The route here is the free-group Hall-Witt identity
`gThreeFour_hallWitt`, proved by `group`, followed by two reductions valid in `Q₄`: a triple
bracket lies in `Z`, so conjugating it does nothing (`gThreeFour_conj_triple`), and an inverse
in the middle slot of a triple bracket is invisible (`gThreeFour_triple_inv_mid`).

**Both are stated as standing tooling, not as local steps.**  `gThreeFour_hallWitt` is an
identity in an arbitrary group: no topology, no level, no class, closed by `group`.  And
`gThreeFour_jacobi_of` isolates the passage from Hall-Witt to Jacobi as an implication over an
arbitrary group, taking the two reductions as hypotheses.  So a run at any other layer supplies
its own two facts and gets Jacobi without touching the word.  `gThreeFour_jacobi` is then the
`γ₃/γ₄` instance, and it is the only part of this section specific to class four.

## §3 The layer is generated by squares and brackets of `Y`

⭐⭐ `gThreeFour_zLayer_le`: a **closed** subgroup of `Q₄` containing `y ^ 2` for every `y ∈ Y`
and `commP y q` for every `y ∈ Y` and every `q` contains all of `Z`.  This is the tower form of
`γ₃/γ₄ = V^{[4]} ⊕ (Λ²V)^{[2]} ⊕ L₃(V)`: it is exactly the "generated by" half, which is the
half a witness construction consumes, and it is what `Levelwise`'s `zLayer_two_le_of_gens` does
one class down.  The proof is the recursion `γ₃ = twoCentralSucc γ₂` pulled back along
`levelMk`, so the closedness hypothesis is what pays for the topological closure in
`twoCentralSucc`.

Lane W70-CFOUR.  New file; no edits to existing files.  Prefix `gThreeFour`.
-/

open Multiplicative

namespace GQ2

open Roe.Labute

namespace Dyadic

namespace SqCore

